implement a friend group that is accessed from the profile screen. allow people to join a group by showing a QR code and the other person scans the code or by email address, when sharing by email address the other user would need to confirm the friend request. This confirmation request should be shown in push notifications and shown in app on launch with a snackbar and on the profile screen
The push notification permission request should be moved to a screen in the FTUE and the reason for the request be given as supporting downloading (if browse is enabled) and friend requests.

if a user is in a friend group add an option to the long press of a book in the list and the player overflow menu to sync listen with one or more users. sync listen will have two modes. 1) all playback operations of one user are mirrored to all other users (play/pause/seek/etc) 2) the position of all users is tracked and shared and visible in a dialog on the player. in either mode there is also an option in the playback menu to send a message to all members of the shared playback group. There should be an element that can be added to skins to open the shared playback dialog

Streak/finish-count/badge stats and the actively-used statistics/daily and statistics/timeline endpoints were migrated off the dead listening_statistics table onto the event-sourced listening_events/book_positions tables (ListeningActivityService, BadgeService, StatisticsController::getOverview/getDailyStatsOpenApi/getTimelineStats). While investigating, also found and fixed a real duplicate-event bug: sessions:close-orphaned couldn't recognize its own previously-synthesized SESSION_END events (device_id mismatch), so every scheduled rerun added another duplicate for the same orphan, unbounded - some users had 96-145 duplicate rows for one real session, inflating daily listening past 24h. Fixed the recognition bug, fixed its metadata key mismatch (was writing session_duration_ms, nothing read it), and added defensive dedup-by-(user,book,device,timestamp) in ListeningActivityService::getSessions() so any future resync duplicates (real client retries, not just the orphan-closer) can't inflate totals again.

Remaining: StatisticsController::getWeeklyStats, getBookStats, getListeningTrends, getTopBooks, getDashboardStats (its today/all-time totals, not the listening_minutes breakdown which is now migrated) still read the legacy listening_statistics table directly - lower priority since no current client calls these routes (confirmed via grep across the client repo), but still worth migrating or removing as dead code. BadgeService::getLibrarySize still can't see purely-local unplayed books since there's no client sync signal for "this book exists in my local library" independent of play activity/status. Next task: audit the codebase for remaining reads/writes against listening_statistics (and any other tables no longer populated by current clients) and migrate or remove them, the same way this pass did for streaks/badges/timeline.

Multi-directory book storage (support BOOK_STORAGE_PATH being a list of roots, not one path), picked up 2026-08-16, not started. Investigation found the codebase resolves the storage root two competing ways: the "correct" seam App\Services\BookPathService::getBookRoot() (config('filesystems.disks.books.root') ?? config('app.book_root'), validated as a real directory) has only 3 callers (BookFilesystemService, LibraryRepairService, itself); everything else (~44 files - controllers, console commands, jobs, BookImportTrait, BookImportService, BookDirectoryParser, GenreMappingService, etc.) reads config('app.book_root') directly, bypassing the service, several duplicating the "not configured" error inline. books.directory_path in the DB is stored relative to the single root (per comments in BookImportTrait/BookDirectoryParser), so multi-root breaks unless each book also records which root it belongs to. config/library_profiles.php already has an adjacent but different multi-path concept: it picks one book_storage_path per request based on hostname via ResolveLibraryProfileFromHost middleware (select-one-by-host, not search-across-many). Callers that assume exactly one root and would need to loop over all configured roots instead: CreateImportJobsForDirectory/ImportBooksFromDownloads (directory scanning for new imports), BookImportService::checkDiskSpace and ApiHealthController (disk_free_space checks), DirectoryBrowserController/DirectoryValidationController (admin path browsing/validation), LibraryRepairService.

Planned sequencing: (1) add a nullable storage_root/library_id column to books, non-destructive migration, backfilled to the current single root; (2) change BOOK_STORAGE_PATH to accept a list (e.g. BOOK_STORAGE_PATHS, comma or JSON), keep the old singular env var as a backward-compat alias resolving to a one-element list; (3) expand BookPathService into the mandatory single source of truth - getBookRoots(): array, resolveBookRoot(?string $storageKey): string, findBookRootContaining(string $absolutePath): ?string; (4) migrate the ~44 direct-config() callers onto BookPathService, in reviewable batches grouped by concern (scanning/import, serving/reading, disk-space/health, admin path-editing, CLI maintenance commands) rather than one sweep; (5) fix the "assumes one root" consumers listed above to iterate all configured roots; (6) decide how a book's root is chosen at import time for new books (first root with space? user-selectable?). Per CLAUDE.md's UNTESTABLE_REGRESSIONS.md rule, this touches real filesystem paths broadly across scanning/serving - extend that doc for the new multi-root paths when this is implemented, since they can't be covered by automated tests.

getOverview()'s remaining fields (total_listening_time_ms, books_started, average_session_duration_ms, favorite_genres, daily_stats, listening_minutes) were still reading the dead listening_statistics table even after the above migration - found live in production (client showed mostly-zero overview data). Migrated the rest onto ListeningActivityService. Also found and fixed a real arithmetic bug while investigating a "longest_streak always shows 1" report: Carbon 3's diffInDays() returns a signed float, and ListeningActivityService::getLongestStreak() compared it against the literal int 1, which never matched for ascending-sorted dates (the value comes back negative) - longest_streak was silently pinned at 1 for every user regardless of real history. Fixed by comparing the absolute day count, and hardened the same pattern in BadgeService::getQuickFinishCount().
